  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThe 00Z Topeka sounding shows a saturated layer from near the\nsurface up through the lowest few thousand feet aloft, capped by a\nsharp inversion with very dry air above. RRFS, HRRR, and RAP\nforecast soundings all hold that inversion through this afternoon\nwith the boundary layer still stratus-favored at peak heating, so\nthe deck has no obvious path to mix out from below. Water vapor\nimagery confirms the dry column aloft with surface high pressure\nover the central Plains. Northwest flow aloft holds across the\narea between a ridge over the northern Plains and the trough that\nexited into the Great Lakes, keeping subsidence and dry air in\nplace above the stratus deck. Any lingering drizzle shifts\nsoutheast of the area before sunrise. Cloud cover likely\nsuppresses highs into the low 50s this afternoon and holds lows up\nearly this morning without efficient radiational cooling.\nConfidence in a cloudy, cool day today is high.\n\nThe real question is when the deck breaks, and the answer is not\nin total agreement among guidance. The RRFS is slow to clear the\nstratus even into Saturday morning, and the global ensembles show\nwide spreads on cloud cover, with ENS, GEFS, and GEPS members split\nbetween clearing this evening and holding clouds past sunrise\nSaturday before every member clears by Sunday. The stratus should\nfinally erode Saturday afternoon as the surface high moves east\nand deeper mixing wins out, but how fast that happens overnight is\nthe uncertainty that drives Saturday morning lows. Clear skies and\nlight winds under the high would bring efficient radiational\ncooling and lows near freezing, while lingering clouds would keep\nlows in the mid 30s, so the temperature spread Saturday morning\nhinges on how much cloud cover remains. Given the overall\nuncertainty in cloud coverage, a drop into the low 30s is possible\nbut not a slam dunk, with the best chance for a brief early\nmorning freeze in the usual cold spots and no sign of a hard\nfreeze. This is still the first morning worth watching for\nsensitive plants left out considering the unusual extended growing\nseason.\n\nSunday through Wednesday still looks quiet and mild. Height plumes\nclimb steadily into Monday with very tight ensemble agreement,\nplacing the area under broad ridging with no meaningful synoptic\nforcing giving way to near-zero rain chances through at least\nmidweek. Return flow helps to elevate highs into the upper 60s to\nnear 70, well above normal for early November, with lows\nrecovering into the 40s. Confidence in the dry, warm stretch is\nhigh given the tight height spread.\n\nThe ridge flattens late next week as ensemble spreads widen beyond\nWednesday. Ensembles agree a trough digs into the West but differ\non its amplitude and on whether a cold front reaches the area by\nThursday or holds off until the weekend. Until that spread\nnarrows, the forecast stays with the blend, keeping temperatures\nmild and rain chances low, with the next real pattern change\nwaiting on that front.",
